2025-05-01 03:04:19 - Thread-1182 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• player.debut : first MLB game date – starting point of the span  
• player.final_game : last MLB game date – ending point of the span  

Both columns are in ISO format ‘YYYY‑MM‑DD’, so we can pull the year, month and day pieces with substr().  
Condition that will be used in most queries: both debut and final_game must be non‑null and have the full 10‑character date string (length = 10).

Below are ten exploratory / step‑by‑step SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows) that go from very simple inspection to the final computation of the required average career‑span value.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: Take a peek at raw debut and final_game values.
SELECT DISTINCT player_id, debut, final_game
FROM player
WHERE debut <> '' AND final_game <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: How many players actually have both dates filled?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS players_with_both_dates
FROM player
WHERE debut <> '' AND final_game <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Extract the year part of debut and final_game to see the spread.
SELECT DISTINCT 
       substr(debut, 1, 4)  AS debut_year,
       substr(final_game, 1, 4) AS final_year
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Show the basic year difference (final – debut) for a sample of players.
SELECT player_id,
       CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
       CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)  AS year_diff
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Pull year, month, day pieces separately for later use.
SELECT player_id,
       CAST(substr(debut,1,4) AS INTEGER)  AS debut_year,
       CAST(substr(debut,6,2) AS INTEGER)  AS debut_month,
       CAST(substr(debut,9,2) AS INTEGER)  AS debut_day,
       CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER)  AS final_year,
       CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER)  AS final_month,
       CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER)  AS final_day
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Compute raw signed differences (years, months, days).
SELECT player_id,
       (CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)) AS diff_years,
       (CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr(debut,6,2)      AS INTEGER)) AS diff_months,
       (CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr(debut,9,2)      AS INTEGER)) AS diff_days
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Turn those signed differences into absolute values and
--                 round each component to two decimals.
SELECT player_id,
       ROUND(ABS(
             CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
             CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)
       ), 2) AS abs_years,
       ROUND(ABS(
             CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER) -
             CAST(substr(debut,6,2)      AS INTEGER)
       ), 2) AS abs_months,
       ROUND(ABS(
             CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER) -
             CAST(substr(debut,9,2)      AS INTEGER)
       ), 2) AS abs_days
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Compute each player’s career‑span value
--                 (abs_years + abs_months/12 + abs_days/365).
SELECT player_id,
       ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
                 CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)), 2)           +
       ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER) -
                 CAST(substr(debut,6,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 12.0    +
       ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER) -
                 CAST(substr(debut,9,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 365.0     AS career_span
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Verify the distribution – list 20 largest career spans.
SELECT player_id, career_span
FROM (
  SELECT player_id,
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)), 2)           +
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,6,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 12.0    +
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,9,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 365.0   AS career_span
  FROM player
  WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
)
ORDER BY career_span DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final query – average single career span for all players.
SELECT ROUND(AVG(
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,1,4) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,1,4)      AS INTEGER)), 2)           +
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,6,2) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,6,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 12.0    +
         ROUND(ABS(CAST(substr(final_game,9,2) AS INTEGER) -
                   CAST(substr(debut,9,2)      AS INTEGER)), 2) / 365.0
       ), 2) AS avg_career_span_years
FROM player
WHERE length(debut)=10 AND length(final_game)=10
LIMIT 20;
```

The last statement (Query 10) produces the requested float: the average career‑span in years for all players, built exactly according to the step‑by‑step specification.
